//
// Summary:
//     Encapsulates the exception handling logic.
//
// Parameters:
//   state:
//     The current PexRunSettings.
//
// Returns:
//     The updated PexRunSettings.
static PexRunSettings HandleExceptions(PexRunSettings state)
{
    state.ExceptionHandlingMode = PexExceptionHandlingMode.Rethrow;
    return state;
}
//
// Summary:
//     Called when the method UnderTest is executed successfully.
//
// Parameters:
//   args:
//     The tuple of method UnderTest input arguments.
//
// Returns:
//     The expected method UnderTest return value.
static System.Tuple`2<bool,ArrayList> TestMethodSuccessObserver(System.Tuple`2<ArrayList, int> args)
{
    return new System.Tuple<bool, ArrayList>(true, args.Item1);
}

//
// Summary:
//     Called when method UnderTest execution will fail.
//
// Parameters:
//   args:
//     The tuple of method UnderTest input arguments.
//
// Returns:
//     The expected method UnderTest return value if it's possible to predictably handle the exception, or false in other cases.
static System.Tuple`2<bool,ArrayList> TestMethodExceptionObserver(System.Tuple`2<ArrayList, int> args)
{
    return new System.Tuple<bool, ArrayList>(true, args.Item1);
}
//
// Summary:
//     The method call under test.
//
// Parameters:
//   testDelegate:
//     The method to be measured.
//
// Returns:
//     The method under test output.
public static MethodReturn RunTestMethod(TestDelegate testDelegate)
{
    PexRunner pexRunner = new PexRunner(testDelegate, HandleExceptions, TestMethodSuccessObserver, TestMethodExceptionObserver);
    PexRunResult result = pexRunner.Run();
    if (!result.IsSuccess)
    {
        Console.WriteLine(result.ToVerboseString());
    }
    return result.MethodReturn;
}

I am getting a warning about the RunTestMethod being deprecated. I do not know why. Maybe you can help me?
My concern is that there seems to be a problem with the PexRunner, and I cannot run my test method. I have followed the exact instructions in the book I am following.

A: The RunTestMethod is deprecated and has been removed in Pex 4.0.

Instead of PexRunner and RunTestMethod , starting with Pex 4.0, you would use the static framework methods directly without creating any PexRunner instances :


*

*PexRunner.Explore - to explore your code and generate--------------------        }
    }
}


A: In .NET, the ArrayList indexOf and lastIndexOf implementations don't work as expected. Both methods start to search from the first (or last of the array if a flag is used) element and stop when a match is found. Neither, however, take possible indexed exception or index being beyond the range.
Here is an example of the problem:
var arrList = new ArrayList();
arrList.Add(1);
arrList.Add(2);
arrList.Add(3);
...
Console.WriteLine("Index of 2: {0}", arrList.IndexOf(2)); //prints 1
...
arrList[1] = 2;
Console.WriteLine("Index of 2: {0}", arrList.IndexOf(2)); //still prints 1
...
arrList[1] = 3;
Console.WriteLine("Index of 3: {0}", arrList.IndexOf(3)); //prints 2
...

The above code will always return the index of the first occurrence of the specified object in the entire ArrayList, regardless of whether you use IndexOf or LastIndexOf.
This behavior is different from List<T> where lastIndexOf method correctly returns the index of the last occurrence.
Lastly, NUnit's assert class is called "Assert", so you should use the Assert.AreEqual method to compare actual and expected values in your tests.
Also, you said you want to compare expected and actual values but there is no expected value in your contracts. I assume you meant expected behavior which we cannot see from your code.
P.S If you want to get rid of the ArrayList. Try using List<T> it's much more efficient and safer, in terms of type safety and performance. If you cannot change API you are using, look into IList interface which will provide you what you need and be able to cast it to List<T>. 
P.P.S Your contracts are all written based on the assumption that the ArrayList will behave in a certain way. This assumption is not guaranteed by the ArrayList class, so if things do not act under that assumption, your code could break or give unexpected results.
Q: How can I make an app with react-native and share info through bluetooth? I'm currently trying to make an app that uses React Native and has the ability to share data through Bluetooth. 
Is there any tutorial or guide that can guide me through the process of setting up the environment, learning to use specific modules, and making an application that can do Bluetooth data sharing? 
Thanks in advance..

A: Unfortunately, React Native does not currently have a built-in module for Bluetooth functionality until recently. Some libraries have been developed but they are not widely supported nor are they updated regularly.
However, there is a package for Bluetooth LE peripheral functionality in react-native that you can use (react-native-ble-peripheral), and it's updated a lot recently.
Here is how you can use this package:


*

*Firstly, you need to install it via npm or yarn:


```
npm install react-native-ble-peripheral --save
or
yarn add react-native-ble-peripheral
```

After this, it's advisable to link the module with your project by following the guide here:
https://github.com/innoveit/react-native-ble-peripheral
Beyond this, you will need to know JavaScript/ES6, Bluetooth LE standards, and a good understanding of asynchronous programming. 
Remember, Bluetooth is a complex standard and some functionalities can't be implemented/accessed unless the device is a central or peripheral Bluetooth device.
Remember to have your android and ios environment setup properly with Bluetooth usage permissions as well.

A: As per the current version of react-native, Bluetooth modules are not natively supported, or to be more precise they have been supported for a while but not in a mainstream or recommended way. 
Most of the Bluetooth-specific functionalities are to be handled in the underlying mobile operating system using Native Modules(Objective-C/Swift for iOS, Java/Kotlin for Android).
React-Native has a bridging layer to interact with these Native Modules, which allows you to work with BLE(Bluetooth Low Energy) in certain ways but for full control it's typically easier to use the Native Code and leverage libraries such as react-native-ble-manager.

The steps would typically involve the following:


*

*Follow the React-Native Ble-Manager library installation steps.

